DEVLOG #3 — Parsing, Error Handling & First Frontend

After getting the first working PageSpeed API integration in the previous devlog, today I focused on making the backend more useful and reliable.

The main goal was to stop returning the entire PageSpeed response and instead extract only the information SiteScope AI actually needs.

1. PageSpeed response parsing

The PageSpeed Insights API returns a large JSON response containing many different sections and values.

I created simplify_pagespeed_response() to process this response.

The function currently extracts:

Category scores
Performance
SEO
Accessibility
Best Practices

The Lighthouse API returns scores as values between 0 and 1, so I convert them to a 0–100 scale.

For example:

{
  "performance": 100,
  "accessibility": 96,
  "best-practices": 96,
  "seo": 80
}
Audit opportunities

From the Lighthouse audits section, I extract audits where the score indicates that there is something to improve.

Each opportunity contains:

{
  "id": "...",
  "title": "...",
  "description": "...",
  "severity": "...",
  "displayValue": "..."
}

This makes the response much smaller and easier for the frontend and future AI processing to work with.

2. Severity classification

I also added a simple severity classification based on the Lighthouse audit score.

Current thresholds:

score < 0.5  → critical
score < 0.9  → warning
score >= 0.9 → minor

The opportunities are then sorted by severity so the most important problems appear first.

This is only an initial classification system. Later I may replace or improve it depending on how SiteScope AI evaluates different types of issues.

3. Error handling

A working API integration also needs to handle situations where something goes wrong.

I added handling for several possible failures:

PageSpeed API timeout
Connection errors
Other HTTP/network request errors
Invalid or inaccessible websites
PageSpeed API rate limits
Unexpected API status codes
Invalid JSON responses

Instead of allowing the backend to crash, the API now returns an appropriate HTTP error response with a human-readable message.

For example, a timeout returns HTTP 504, while a rate-limit response returns HTTP 429.

4. Testing the endpoint

I tested the backend locally using curl.

Example request:

curl.exe -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/audit \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"url":"https://example.com"}'

The endpoint successfully returned a simplified audit response containing the website URL, category scores and detected opportunities.

This confirmed that the basic pipeline is working:

URL
 ↓
FastAPI
 ↓
PageSpeed Insights API
 ↓
Lighthouse response
 ↓
SiteScope AI parser
 ↓
Simplified JSON
